1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,680
In a few agentic applications I've worked on, I gave the Elm the option to write code to then carry out the task I wanted it to, and I've been a few times now, I've been really surprised and delighted by the cleverness of the code solutions it generated in order to solve various tasks for me.

2
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:26,460
So, if you haven't used code execution much, I think you might be surprised and delighted at what this will let your Elm applications do.

3
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:27,640
Let's take a look.

4
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:35,420
Let's take an example of building an application that can input math word problems and solve them for you.

5
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:40,420
So, you might create tools to add numbers, subtract numbers, multiply numbers, and divide numbers.

6
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:46,980
And if someone says, please add 13.2 plus 18.9, then it triggers the add2 and then it gets you the right answer.

7
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,100
But when someone now types in, what is the square root of 2?

8
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:53,940
Well, one thing you could do is write a new tool for a square root.

9
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:58,300
But then maybe some new thing is needed to carry out exponentiation.

10
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:03,420
And in fact, if you look at the number of buttons on your modern scientific calculator,

11
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:09,900
are you going to create a separate tool for every one of these buttons and the many more things that we would want to do in math calculation?

12
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:18,920
So, instead of trying to implement one tool after another, a different approach is to let it write and execute code.

13
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:22,000
To tell the Elm to write code, you might write a prompt like this.

14
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:23,940
Write code to solve the user's query.

15
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:30,580
Return the answer as Python code delimited with execute Python and closing execute Python tags.

16
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,860
So, given a query like, what is the square root of 2?

17
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,360
The Elm might generate output like this.

18
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:40,040
You can then use pattern matching, for example a regular expression,

19
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:46,520
to look for the start and end execute Python tags and extract the code in between.

20
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:50,200
So, here you get these two lines of code shown in the green box.

21
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:57,580
And you can then execute this code for the Elm and get the output, in this case 1.4142 and so on.

22
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:06,160
Lastly, this numerical answer is then passed back to the Elm and it can write a nicely formatted answer to the original question.

23
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:10,260
There are a few different ways you can carry out the code execution step for the Elm.

24
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:13,380
One is to use Python's exec function.

25
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:17,820
This is a built-in Python function, which will execute whatever code you pass in.

26
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:24,000
And this is very powerful for your Elm to really write code and get you to execute that code.

27
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:28,700
Although there are some security implications, which we'll see later in this video.

28
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:34,440
And then there are also some tools that will let you run the code in a safer sandbox environment.

29
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:38,260
And of course, square root of 2 is a relatively simple example.

30
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:48,160
An Elm can also accurately write code to, for example, do interest calculations and solve much harder math calculations than this.

31
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:55,420
One refinement to this idea, which you sort of saw in our section on reflection, is that if code execution fails,

32
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:59,760
So if for some reason the Elm had generated code that wasn't quite correct,

33
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:05,600
then passing that error message back to the Elm to let it reflect and maybe revise this code

34
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:11,560
and try another one or two times, that can sometimes also allow it to get a more accurate answer.

35
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:19,700
Now, running arbitrary code that an Elm generates does have a small chance of causing something bad to happen.

36
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:30,040
Recently, one of my team members was using a highly agentic coder and it actually chose to remove star.py within a project directory.

37
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:34,160
So it was actually a real example. And eventually that agentic coder did apologize.

38
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:36,900
It said, yes, that's actually right. That was an incredibly stupid mistake.

39
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:43,340
I guess I was glad that this agentic coder was really sorry, but I already deleted a bunch of Python files.

40
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:45,980
Unfortunately, the team member had it backed up with GitHub repo.

41
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:51,480
So there was no real harm done, but it would have been not great if this, you know, arbitrary code,

42
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:56,180
which made the mistake of deleting a bunch of files, had been executed without a backup.

43
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:01,180
So the best practice for code execution is to run it inside a sandbox environment.

44
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:06,040
In practice, the risk for any single line of code is not that high.

45
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:11,460
So if I'm being candid, many developers will execute code from the Elm without too much checking.

46
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:20,080
But if you want to be a bit safer, then the best practice is to create a sandbox so that if an Elm generates bad code,

47
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:24,920
there's a lower risk of data loss or leakage of sensitive data and so on.

48
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:38,940
So sandbox environments like Docker or E2B as a lightweight sandbox environment can reduce the risk of arbitrary code being executed in a way that damages your system or your environment.

49
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:50,360
It turns out that code execution is so important that a lot of trainers of Elms actually do special work to make sure that code execution works well on their applications.

50
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:59,540
But I hope that as you add this as one more tool for you to potentially offer to Elms or let you make your applications much more powerful.

51
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:07,920
So far in what we've discussed, you have to create tools and make them available one at a time to your Elm.

52
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:17,660
It turns out that many different teams are building similar tools and having to do all this work of building functions and making them available to the Elms.

53
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:29,740
But there is recently a new standard called MCP, Model Context Protocol, that's making it much easier for developers to get access to a huge set of tools for Elms to use.

54
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:36,180
This is an important protocol that more and more teams are using to develop Elm-based applications.

55
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:39,120
Let's go learn about MCP in the next video.

56
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:39,220
Let's go.

